.TH PROJ 1 "October 2026" "proj 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
proj \- manage project development environments with Lmod
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B proj
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B proj
is a metadata-driven project environment manager for Linux with Zsh or Bash.
It coordinates Lmod modules, optional micromamba, conda, or mamba
environments, project
variables, paths, aliases, and lifecycle hooks.
It does not replace Git, Lmod, or Python environment management.
.PP
Managed metadata is stored in
.IR ~/.proj/envs/ name /project.toml
and points to the actual project root.
A shared installation adds a group registry whose environments every group
member can activate.
The active project is local to the current shell.
.SH QUICK START
.nf
cd /path/to/atlas
proj create atlas
proj activate atlas
proj config project.description="Atlas development"
proj set-python-env atlas
proj module add compiler gcc/14
.fi
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "proj create " name " [--root " directory "] [--shared [--group-writable]]"
Register a project. The root defaults to the current directory. Creates a
valid starter document in the private registry,
prints its location, and leaves optional fields commented.
.B --shared
creates it in the shared registry instead; only its creator may change it
unless
.B --group-writable
is also given. Members cannot remove environments they do not own.
.TP
.B proj list
List registered project environments with their scope and owner. A private
environment shadows a shared one with the same name. Invalid documents are
reported and skipped.
.TP
.BI "proj activate " name
Load the generated project Lmod module, activate the configured Python
environment, change to the project root, and run startup hooks.
This command requires the shell integration. Activating an environment owned
by someone else prints a notice: its hooks and aliases run as you.
.TP
.B proj deactivate
Reverse the activation performed in the current shell. This also works when
the environment was removed while active.
.TP
.BR "proj remove " name " [--yes] [--delete-files | --keep-files]"
Delete an environment's
.IR project.toml
after asking for confirmation. Other files in its directory and its generated
modulefile are listed, and deleted only if confirmed or with
.BR --delete-files ;
.B --yes
alone keeps them. The project root and Python environment are never touched.
The environment active in the current shell cannot be removed, and only its
owner can remove a shared environment. Without a terminal,
.B --yes
is required.
.TP
.B proj current
Print the active project name. Returns nonzero when none is active.
.TP
.B proj status
Display the active project and report module or Python environment drift.
.TP
.BR "proj info " "[--env " name "]"
Render effective project metadata. With no option, select the active project
environment. The Zsh integration uses the dots bordered panel when available.
.TP
.BR "proj config " "[--env " name "] " key [ = value ]
Read or write metadata. Without
.I --env
the active project is selected. Values use TOML syntax; unquoted text is
treated as a string. Environment-affecting changes take effect after
reactivation.
.TP
.BR "proj set-python-env " "[--env " name "] [--manager " manager "] " environment
Validate and link an existing environment of
.BR micromamba ,
.BR conda ,
or
.BR mamba ,
by name or by absolute prefix path. The manager defaults to the project's,
then to the configured
.BR python_manager . A shared environment records the prefix so every member activates
the same Python environment.
.B proj
does not create the Python environment.
.TP
.BR "proj configmenu " "[--env " name "] [--setup]"
Edit an environment in a full-screen menu: fixed facts and editable settings
on the left, the resulting
.I project.toml
with unsaved lines marked on the right. Dialogs list live choices (Python
managers, environments, Lmod modules) with a Rescan button. Ctrl+S saves;
Escape closes a dialog unchanged, and on the main screen asks before
discarding unsaved changes, defaulting to staying.
.B --setup
installs the menu's private runtime (Textual) under
.IR PREFIX /libexec/menu ;
it needs network access.
.TP
.BR "proj python-env list " "[--manager " manager ]
List environments reported by the default or named manager.
.TP
.BR "proj module " "{list|add|remove} " category " [" module "]"
Manage module dependencies. Categories are
.BR project ,
.BR toolchain ,
.BR application ,
and
.BR compiler .
The active project is selected unless
.I --env
is supplied.
.TP
.B proj env list
List generated Lmod project modules and whether each cache entry is current.
.TP
.BI "proj env show " name
Show validated metadata and the effective generated environment.
.TP
.B proj doctor
Check shell integration, Lmod, the Python manager, site configuration, the shared
registry, metadata, and writable cache state.
.TP
.BR "proj init " "[--shell zsh|bash] [--rc " file "] [--reverse]"
Add a marked block that sources the shell integration to a startup file:
.I ~/.zshrc
or
.I ~/.bashrc
by default, detected from
.BR SHELL .
Rerunning replaces the block;
.B --reverse
removes it.
.TP
.B proj install
Idempotently link the command, manual, and TLDR page into the
.IR bin " and " share
directories beside the checkout (for example
.I /tools/bin
for
.IR /tools/proj ).
Existing conflicting paths are never overwritten. Use
.I install.sh
for a standalone installation.
.TP
.B proj --version
Print the installed proj version.
.SH CONFIGURATION KEYS
Generic configuration supports:
.PP
.nf
project.description     project.root
project.shell           python.manager
python.environment      paths.prepend
hooks.startup           hooks.shutdown
modules.<category>      environment.<variable>
aliases.<name>
.fi
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
proj config project.description="Research tools"
proj config environment.PROJECT_MODE=development
proj config paths.prepend='["${project.root}/bin"]'
proj config aliases.project-root='cd ${project.root}'
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR ~/.proj/envs/ name /project.toml
Managed project metadata.
.TP
.IR PREFIX /etc/config.toml
Site configuration written by the installer:
.BR registry ,
.BR shared_registry ,
.BR group ,
and
.BR python_manager .
.TP
.I ~/.config/proj/config.toml
Optional user configuration; overrides site keys.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/proj/modulefiles
Generated Lmod modulefile cache.
.TP
.IR PREFIX /shell/init.zsh ", " PREFIX /shell/init.bash
Shell integration that permits activation to modify the parent shell.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PROJ_ACTIVE
Name of the active project.
.TP
.B PROJ_HOME
Override the managed registry home. Defaults to
.IR ~/.proj .
.TP
.B PROJ_CONFIG
Override the user configuration file.
.TP
.B PROJ_SITE_CONFIG
Override the site configuration file.
.TP
.BR PROJ_SHARED_REGISTRY ", " PROJ_GROUP
Override the shared registry and its group.
.TP
.B PROJ_PYTHON_MANAGER
Override the default Python manager.
.TP
.B PROJ_PROJECT_ROOTS
Colon-separated additional metadata roots.
.TP
.B PROJ_CACHE_HOME
Override the cache directory.
.TP
.B PROJ_GLOBAL_BIN
Override the public command directory during installation and shell setup.
.TP
.B PROJ_GLOBAL_MAN
Override the public manual root during installation and shell setup.
.SH COMPLETION
The Bash adapter registers a completion function. The Zsh adapter adds its
completion directory to
.B fpath
and supports both earlier and later
.B compinit
ordering. Commands, project names, configuration keys, module categories, and
Python environments are completed. Metadata discovery is deferred until
completion is requested.
.PP
For module changes, completion proceeds from action to category to module.
The
.B add
action offers modules currently available through Lmod; the
.B remove
action offers modules configured in that category for the selected project.
Proj categories organize metadata and do not claim that Lmod provides a
universal category taxonomy.
.SH NOTES
Lmod remains authoritative for environment mutation and uses the
.B project
family to enforce one active project environment.
The project's Python manager remains authoritative for Python environments.
Task metadata may be displayed, but
.B proj run
is not implemented.
.PP
When dots logging functions are available, lifecycle messages use their
colored success, information, and error styles. Otherwise proj uses a
standalone Unicode fallback.
.PP
.B --project
is retained as a compatibility alias for
.BR --env .
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. Status 1 is used for negative queries such as no active
project. Status 2 indicates invalid configuration, unavailable integration, or
unsafe state.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR zsh (1),
.BR bash (1),
.BR module (1),
.BR micromamba (1),
.BR conda (1)
